1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,742
We're CodingClaws: Nagur,
Sahith, Pranav, and Geethanjali.

2
00:00:04,782 --> 00:00:06,520
And this is PathPro.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:10,370
Pedestrian deaths in Atlanta
are a public-health problem.

4
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:15,211
Many of us walk between Klaus, Tech
Square, and Midtown MARTA late at night.

5
00:00:15,251 --> 00:00:19,019
Our friends, especially women,
already plan routes around well-lit,

6
00:00:19,059 --> 00:00:22,468
busier streets, and text each
other when they get home.

7
00:00:22,508 --> 00:00:26,973
Yet every navigation app asks
one question: what's fastest?

8
00:00:27,013 --> 00:00:30,572
So we built PathPro: one map
of the risks on your way,

9
00:00:30,612 --> 00:00:34,345
from traffic and darkness to
reported crimes and street hazards,

10
00:00:34,385 --> 00:00:37,184
with better tools for getting
around alone at night.

11
00:00:37,224 --> 00:00:39,866
Map apps optimize for time.

12
00:00:39,906 --> 00:00:42,292
Crime maps label whole neighborhoods.

13
00:00:42,332 --> 00:00:45,241
Location-sharing apps
don't plan your route.

14
00:00:45,281 --> 00:00:47,621
PathPro does it together, honestly:

15
00:00:47,661 --> 00:00:51,650
a crash-trained model that beats
the City's own High Injury Network,

16
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:55,456
explains every score, and never
routes around neighborhoods.

17
00:00:55,496 --> 00:00:59,032
Open pathpro.tech in any phone browser.

18
00:00:59,072 --> 00:01:01,772
There's no app to install and no login.

19
00:01:01,812 --> 00:01:06,311
Two taps take you from opening it to a
route that starts at your GPS location.

20
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:08,134
It works on desktop too,

21
00:01:08,174 --> 00:01:11,861
with keyboard and screen-reader
labels and large touch targets,

22
00:01:11,901 --> 00:01:14,729
and your walking patterns
stay on your phone.

23
00:01:14,769 --> 00:01:18,444
Here's Klaus to Midtown MARTA at
10:30 on a rainy Friday night.

24
00:01:18,484 --> 00:01:23,039
The teal PathPro route is about four
minutes longer than the fastest walk,

25
00:01:23,079 --> 00:01:26,175
with 54% less traffic risk.

26
00:01:26,268 --> 00:01:30,042
Tap Why, and it names the
high-risk stretches it avoids.

27
00:01:30,082 --> 00:01:33,629
Tap Listen, and ElevenLabs reads it aloud.

28
00:01:33,869 --> 00:01:40,050
<i>The PathPro route adds 4.2 minutes</i>
<i>and cuts traffic-risk exposure 54%…</i>

29
00:01:40,099 --> 00:01:43,252
Start navigation, and
PathPro walks with you,

30
00:01:43,292 --> 00:01:46,655
speaking up before each
high-traffic-risk crossing.

31
00:01:46,695 --> 00:01:50,811
<i>High traffic risk ahead. Fowler Street</i>
<i>Northwest and Ferst Drive Northwest,</i>

32
00:01:50,851 --> 00:01:52,754
<i>in 250 m.</i>

33
00:01:52,794 --> 00:01:58,211
On a long walk, like Georgia Tech to Inman
Park, PathPro offers a faster option:

34
00:01:58,251 --> 00:02:02,007
walk 17 minutes to Midtown
station and take MARTA.

35
00:02:02,047 --> 00:02:05,061
Or switch to Bike, E-bike, or Scooter.

36
00:02:05,101 --> 00:02:11,104
This ride takes 27 minutes, with 72%
less traffic risk than the fastest ride.

37
00:02:11,144 --> 00:02:13,356
PathPro shows more than traffic.

38
00:02:13,396 --> 00:02:18,128
It maps reported crimes against
persons: homicide, robbery, and assault,

39
00:02:18,168 --> 00:02:22,598
from Atlanta Police open data,
grouped by area and time of day,

40
00:02:22,638 --> 00:02:25,140
with no addresses or victim details.

41
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:29,959
Bands account for foot traffic, so an area
with no reports is never marked higher,

42
00:02:29,999 --> 00:02:32,455
and a fairness note is always on screen.

43
00:02:32,495 --> 00:02:37,946
Crime is never used to choose your route:
a test multiplies crime counts by 1,000,

44
00:02:37,986 --> 00:02:39,734
and the routes don't change.

45
00:02:39,774 --> 00:02:45,284
Traffic risk comes from vehicle crashes,
speed limits, lanes, traffic volume,

46
00:02:45,324 --> 00:02:50,067
and intersections. It rises
after dark and on wet streets.

47
00:02:50,107 --> 00:02:53,457
Lighting and foot traffic
shape the after-dark option.

48
00:02:53,497 --> 00:02:58,020
People report hazards like blocked
sidewalks, crossing signals that are out,

49
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:01,132
construction, flooding,
and fast-moving traffic.

50
00:03:01,172 --> 00:03:04,616
And for riders, the ride
model accounts for bike lanes.

51
00:03:04,656 --> 00:03:10,456
PathPro is for anyone walking or riding
alone at night: women and girls, students,

52
00:03:10,496 --> 00:03:14,043
people who rely on MARTA
instead of a car, older adults,

53
00:03:14,083 --> 00:03:16,354
and people on bikes and scooters.

54
00:03:16,394 --> 00:03:19,256
Choose well-lit and
busier streets after dark,

55
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:23,087
with help points like Georgia Tech's
blue-light phones along the way.

56
00:03:23,127 --> 00:03:26,547
Share my walk lets a friend follow
along, and if you're running late,

57
00:03:26,587 --> 00:03:30,169
PathPro checks in, with 911 one tap away.

58
00:03:30,209 --> 00:03:33,749
And voice alerts keep your
eyes up, not on the screen.

59
00:03:33,789 --> 00:03:37,364
City Pulse scores all
3,537 areas of the city,

60
00:03:37,404 --> 00:03:40,395
so any address gets a score.

61
00:03:40,622 --> 00:03:44,045
Does it work? We trained on 2020 to 2023,

62
00:03:44,085 --> 00:03:49,212
and tested on 543 pedestrian crashes
from 2024 the model never saw.

63
00:03:49,252 --> 00:03:54,598
The 10% of streets PathPro
ranks highest held seventy-four

64
00:03:54,638 --> 00:03:56,043
percent of them.

65
00:03:56,083 --> 00:03:59,832
The City's High Injury Network: 54%.

66
00:03:59,872 --> 00:04:01,582
Random: 12%.

67
00:04:01,693 --> 00:04:03,731
What does better ranking buy?

68
00:04:03,771 --> 00:04:04,509
For a city,

69
00:04:04,549 --> 00:04:09,687
the same budget spent on 10% of street
length reaches 38% more of next year's

70
00:04:09,727 --> 00:04:12,694
pedestrian crashes than
the High Injury Network,

71
00:04:12,734 --> 00:04:16,909
and, for rides, about
60% more bike crashes.

72
00:04:16,949 --> 00:04:22,121
For a person, four extra minutes cut
traffic-risk exposure by more than half.

73
00:04:22,161 --> 00:04:22,981
Nationally,

74
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:28,356
NHTSA puts the economic cost
of crashes at $340 billion in

75
00:04:28,396 --> 00:04:29,726
2019.

76
00:04:29,766 --> 00:04:35,337
And a new city needs one coverage
polygon, plus its public crash data.

77
00:04:35,377 --> 00:04:39,296
Under the hood, we clean a quarter
million public crash records,

78
00:04:39,336 --> 00:04:44,625
snap them to 50,000 streets, and
score each one by hour and weather.

79
00:04:44,665 --> 00:04:48,725
One bundle feeds a FastAPI
service and a React map.

80
00:04:48,765 --> 00:04:53,741
Vultr runs all of it: the API and
the site, on one VM in Atlanta,

81
00:04:53,781 --> 00:04:58,802
close to our users. Caddy adds
automatic HTTPS, systemd keeps it up,

82
00:04:58,842 --> 00:05:01,146
and one script deploys it.

83
00:05:01,186 --> 00:05:03,840
Our domain is pathpro.tech.

84
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:06,127
Say it out loud: path protect.

85
00:05:06,167 --> 00:05:08,499
The .tech completes the word.

86
00:05:08,539 --> 00:05:11,123
Tiger Data is our system of record:

87
00:05:11,163 --> 00:05:15,860
over 220,000 crash rows
in a Timescale hypertable,

88
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:20,086
a continuous aggregate behind each
street's crashes-by-hour chart,

89
00:05:20,126 --> 00:05:25,517
PostGIS geometry, and a risk
grid of 9.5 million rows.

90
00:05:25,557 --> 00:05:28,768
MongoDB Atlas holds what walkers tell us.

91
00:05:28,808 --> 00:05:33,853
Street reports use a geo index for
map queries, expire after 14 days,

92
00:05:33,893 --> 00:05:37,800
and repeats become confirmations
in one atomic upsert.

93
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,331
Shared walks expire in 6 hours.

94
00:05:40,371 --> 00:05:45,069
Geo plus TTL fits short-lived,
user-generated data.

95
00:05:45,109 --> 00:05:47,368
ElevenLabs gives PathPro its voice,

96
00:05:47,408 --> 00:05:50,985
for explanations and for
walk alerts like this one.

97
00:05:51,025 --> 00:05:55,141
<i>High traffic risk ahead. Fowler Street</i>
<i>Northwest and Ferst Drive Northwest,</i>

98
00:05:55,181 --> 00:05:57,085
<i>in 250 m.</i>

99
00:05:57,125 --> 00:06:02,042
Explanations come from
Groq's GPT-OSS 120B,

100
00:06:02,082 --> 00:06:06,025
for sub-second answers,
with Gemini as the fallback.

101
00:06:06,065 --> 00:06:10,309
A validator rejects any sentence with
a number that isn't in the evidence,

102
00:06:10,349 --> 00:06:13,946
so the LLM never produces a risk number.

103
00:06:13,986 --> 00:06:18,369
That's PathPro: the risks on your
way, for every street, every hour,

104
00:06:18,409 --> 00:06:20,610
and every way you get around Atlanta.

105
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:22,828
Try it at pathpro.tech.

106
00:06:22,868 --> 00:06:25,724
See the risks on your way, before you go.
